[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ yes 1 | ./translation/target/release/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           # never prints, never exits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The same divergence appeared whenever the writer kept the pipe open, even for a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single short line:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ sh -c 'printf "5\n"; sleep 30' | ./c_src/build/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 310                       # exits at once
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ sh -c 'printf "5\n"; sleep 30' | .../translation/target/release/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           # blocked for the full 30s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust `main` drained stdin before scanning:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let mut buf = Vec::new();
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if std::io::stdin().read_to_end(&mut buf).is_ok() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     if let Some(v) = scanf_i32(&buf) { x = v; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `read_to_end` cannot return until the writer closes the pipe. C's `scanf` has no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m such requirement: a `%d` conversion finishes the moment it sees the character
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that terminates the digit run, pushes that character back with `ungetc`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns. It never asks for a byte it does not need, so it is indifferent to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whether stdin is ever closed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that this was invisible to every fixed-input test. Piping a finite buffer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and closing it — which is what a normal test harness does — makes both versions
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m agree, because EOF arrives immediately. Only an open-ended stream exposes it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `scanf_i32` now takes a `BufRead` and pulls bytes lazily through a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `peek`/`consume` pair. `peek` calls `fill_buf`, which is at most one `read`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m syscall and returns a partially filled buffer as soon as data is available, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the scan advances on whatever has arrived rather than waiting for the stream to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m end. The character that terminates the number is peeked but never consumed,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mirroring `scanf`'s `ungetc`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Confirmation.** The fixed version now matches C on all three sub-cases,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m including the ones where C itself blocks — which it must, since it cannot rule
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m out further input:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | C | Rust (after fix) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | endless `"1\n"` | prints `302`, exits 0 | identical |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | endless `"42 "` | prints `384`, exits 0 | identical |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | endless `"y\n"` (matching failure) | prints `300`, exits 0 | identical |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | endless `" "` (never a digit) | blocks forever | blocks forever |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | endless `"1234567890"` (never a delimiter) | blocks forever | blocks forever |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `"5"` with no delimiter, pipe held open | blocks forever | blocks forever |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Regression tests: `endless_stdin_that_terminates_the_number`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `endless_stdin_that_fails_to_match`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `endless_stdin_that_legitimately_blocks`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that were checked and already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are recorded because each is a place where a translation would plausibly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m go wrong, and each is pinned by a test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **`scanf` reads across newlines.** `%d` skips leading whitespace of any amount,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m newlines included, so `"\n\n\n42\n"` converts `42`. A line-oriented reader such
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m as `fgets` would have stopped at the first newline and produced `300`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`scanf_reads_across_newlines_unlike_fgets`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Failed conversion leaves `x` at its initialiser.** `int x = 0;` is written
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before `scanf`, and `scanf` does not store anything on input failure (EOF) or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching failure. Both therefore print `300`, not an error and not a nonzero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit. Inputs in this class: empty stdin, whitespace only, `"abc"`, a lone `"-"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m or `"+"`, `"--1"`, `"."`, a leading NUL or `0xff` byte, and a closed or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unreadable stdin. Note that `"0x10"` lands here only partly — `%d` converts the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0` and leaves `"x10"` unread, giving `300` by a different route.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`matching_failure_leaves_x_at_zero`, `whitespace_only_input`, `empty_input`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No error path exists.** `main` returns `0` unconditionally and nothing writes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to stderr, so every input — valid, malformed or absent — yields exit status 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and empty stderr. The tests assert all three streams on every input rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m just stdout, since a translation that reported malformed input as an error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would otherwise pass.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Leading zeros are decimal.** `%d` is base 10 regardless of a `0` prefix, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"010"` is ten and `"000000000000000000000000000123"` is 123.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`leading_zeros_are_decimal_not_octal`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Conversion stops at the first non-digit and only one number is read.** `"3 4"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `306`, using only the `3`. (`conversion_stops_at_first_non_digit`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **glibc saturates in `long`, then truncates to `int`.** The conversion is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m performed into a `long`; on overflow glibc stores `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` and sets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ERANGE`, and `%d` truncates that `long` to `int`. Two truncations therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stack up, and the results are not intuitive:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | value stored in `x` | printed |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` (`INT_MAX + 1`) | `0` | `300` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967297` (`UINT_MAX + 2`) | `1` | `302` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775808` (`LONG_MAX + 1`) | `-1` (from `LONG_MAX`) | `298` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775809` | `0` (from `LONG_MIN`) | `300` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `999999999999999999999999999999` | `-1` | `298` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust accumulates into an `i64`, clamps to `i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX` on the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m overflow, keeps consuming the remaining digits, and then casts with `as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Digit runs of 64, 1024, 4095, 4096, 4097 and 100 000 characters were checked, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the saturating path is exercised well past any buffer boundary.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`int_boundaries`, `long_boundaries_saturate_then_truncate`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `very_long_digit_runs`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Signed overflow in `driver` wraps.** `2*x` and `y += 300` are `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operations that gcc and clang compile to two's complement wraparound, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x = 1073741674` prints `-2147483648` and `x = INT_MAX` prints `298`. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m uses `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add`; plain `*` and `+` would panic in a debug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build and `saturating_add` gives the wrong answer, which the mutation test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed. `cargo test` runs the debug profile with overflow checks enabled and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m passes, so no unintended overflow remains. The boundary is walked one integer at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a time in both directions. (`arithmetic_overflow_wraps`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `overflow_threshold_sweep`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **`register` is only a hint.** It has no observable effect and is translated as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m an ordinary local.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **`argv` is never read.** Extra command-line arguments change nothing, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests pass argv through to both programs and compare.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`command_line_arguments_are_ignored`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Output format is exactly `"%d\n"`.** One decimal line, no padding or prefix, a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single trailing newline, and nothing on stderr. The C program's output shape is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m asserted literally as `b"300\n"` so that a change in the reference is noticed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rather than silently mirrored. (`output_is_exactly_one_decimal_line`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Write errors are ignored by both.** C does not check the return value of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf`; the Rust discards the `writeln!` result to match. With stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m redirected to `/dev/full` both still exit `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known non-divergence: bytes left unread on stdin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Neither implementation consumes exactly the same number of bytes from the stdin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m file descriptor. glibc fills a stdio buffer of `st_blksize` (typically 4096
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes) and pushes back only the single terminating character, so it over-reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m too; the Rust `BufRead` uses an 8 KiB buffer. The leftover file offset would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only be observable to a process that shares the descriptor and reads after the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m child exits. It affects neither stdout, stderr nor exit status, so it is out of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scope for this comparison — but reading byte-at-a-time to "fix" it would itself
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m be a divergence from glibc, so it was deliberately left alone.
