One real mismatch found and fixed. The Rust translation now matches the C byte-for-byte on stdout, stderr, and exit status across every input class I could construct.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe bug: Rust hung on stdin that never reaches EOF.[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10m$ yes 1 | ./c_src/build/driver
302                       # exits immediately

$ yes 1 | ./translation/target/release/driver
                          # never prints, never exits
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mmain[0m was calling [38;5;10mread_to_end[0m to slurp stdin before scanning. That cannot return until the writer closes the pipe. C's [38;5;10mscanf[0m has no such requirement — a [38;5;10m%d[0m conversion finishes the instant it sees the character terminating the digit run, [38;5;10mungetc[0ms it, and returns, so it never waits on a byte it doesn't need. Even a single short line exposed it: [38;5;10msh -c 'printf "5\n"; sleep 30' | driver[0m printed [38;5;10m310[0m immediately in C and blocked for 30 seconds in Rust.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Notably this was invisible to every fixed-input test. Piping a finite buffer and closing it — what a normal harness does — makes both versions agree, because EOF arrives at once. I only found it in Phase C by asking what happens when stdin stays open.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
